ll with a few  opening paragraphs that explain the  goals the context and set the scene for  what's to follow so like Laura here you  could use an image of cocoa beans to  lead into your flavorful analysis of  chocolate taste readings  explaining your methodology will help  your reader to follow your analysis  through its various steps and this can  be part of your introduction and you can  even go as far as ramshanker did and  build a flowchart out of little  pictograms that explain the different  steps of your analysis and this is  really some Next Level methodology  communication right there  great visuals of course there are they  are at the heart of many Fantastic  notebooks and whether it's for  exploration or for communication you  want your data visualizations to be  clear and accessible in communicating  the insights that you derived  and that can mean simple visuals but it  can also mean complex but well designed  so in this example here Daniel  was creating a visual for climate models  and Rising CO2 levels that would not  look out of place in a science or nature  paper on some high profile website right  that's meticulously designed with insets  and annotations and definitely takes a  little time to digest so like with all  of them if you want to learn more  definitely check out the notebook  but visuals themselves are not enough  right you also want to document your  findings and what you read in those  visuals not just for your audience but  also for yourself so that if you come  back to your notebook maybe six months  later you will thank past you for  writing down what was so interesting  about these plots that you made  right and parole here demonstrates this  very well with her points to note  complete with a little pictogram as a  visual anchor  and documentation is of course the  cousin of narration so you always want  to have a nice narrative flow that leads  your Reader through your analysis from  one step to another  as my final tip for today 